<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Solving for n in permutation equations</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>(a)</b> If \({}_nP_2 = {@pP@}\), find the value of \(n\).</p>
<p>\(n =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p><b>(b)</b> If \({}_nP_3 = {@pP2@}\), find the value of \(n\).</p>
<p>\(n =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Part (a): Solving \({}_nP_2 = {@pP@}\)</b><br>
Use the permutation formula \(\displaystyle {}_nP_r = \frac{n!}{(n - r)!}\) with \(r = 2\) to rewrite the left-hand side:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle {}_nP_2 = \frac{n!}{(n - 2)!} = \frac{n(n - 1)(n - 2)!}{(n - 2)!} = n(n - 1)\)</p>
<p>Set this product equal to \({@pP@}\) and solve the resulting quadratic equation:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle n(n - 1) = {@pP@}\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle n^2 - n - {@pP@} = 0\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle (n - {@pn@})(n + {@pn_m1@}) = 0\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle n = {@pn@} \quad \text{or} \quad n = -{@pn_m1@}\)</p>
<p>Since \(n\) must be an integer with \(n \ge 2\), we reject \(n = -{@pn_m1@}\). Checking the consecutive descending product confirms \({@pn@} \times {@pn_m1@} = {@pP@}\).</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ n = {@ta1@} }\)</p>

<p><b>Part (b): Solving \({}_nP_3 = {@pP2@}\)</b><br>
Use \(\displaystyle {}_nP_r = \frac{n!}{(n - r)!}\) with \(r = 3\) to rewrite the left-hand side:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle {}_nP_3 = \frac{n!}{(n - 3)!} = \frac{n(n - 1)(n - 2)(n - 3)!}{(n - 3)!} = n(n - 1)(n - 2)\)</p>
<p>Find three consecutive descending positive integers (with \(n \ge 3\)) whose product equals \({@pP2@}\):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle n(n - 1)(n - 2) = {@pP2@}\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle {@pn2@} \times {@pn2_m1@} \times {@pn2_m2@} = {@pP2@}\)</p>
<p>Because \(n\) is the largest of the three consecutive integers, \(n = {@ta2@}\).</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ n = {@ta2@} }\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
